IgA Vasculitis (Mild Adult Cutaneous)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Rash on lower extremities post-infection, normal renal function, no joint pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-020
Reason for Visit: Acute development of a non-blanching rash on the legs requiring exclusion of acute meningococcemia or severe thrombocytopenia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden petechial/purpuric rash on the lower limbs. Immediate clinical differentiation from a life-threatening systemic infection (e.g., meningococcal sepsis) or acute bleeding disorder was critical to prevent rapid deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have these dark red bruises all over my shins that won't fade when I press on them, and I'm scared it's that deadly bacterial infection.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports noticing isolated palpable purpura developing over the bilateral lower extremities over the last 12 hours. Patient recovered from a mild upper respiratory tract infection two weeks ago. Tonight, the patient applied pressure to the spots and realized they did not blanch, triggering immense anxiety regarding sepsis. The patient denies fever, rigors, hematuria, melena, or abdominal pain. No joint pain or swelling.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety regarding sepsis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, no signs of systemic toxicity or shock.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals a symmetric, non-blanching, purpuric rash on lower extremities post-infection. Lesions are slightly palpable. No trunk or upper extremity involvement. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are normal. Abdomen is soft and non-tender, indicating no acute bowel ischemia. Urine dipstick is entirely normal (negative for blood and protein, confirming normal renal function clinically).
Environmental Observations: Safe residential setting.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent NSAID or anticoagulant use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: IgA Vasculitis (Mild Adult Cutaneous) - Status: Acute Onset
Differentials:

Meningococcal Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, well appearance, normal heart rate, and lack of systemic toxicity or rapid rash progression to the trunk.

Acute Thrombocytopenic Purpura - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gravity-dependent distribution (legs only) rather than generalized petechiae, and absence of mucosal bleeding.

Systemic Vasculitis with Organ Threat - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal urinalysis (no nephritis) and soft abdomen (no bowel involvement).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a gravity-dependent, non-blanching purpuric rash, a classic red-flag sign necessitating immediate exclusion of meningococcemia or acute coagulopathy. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination. Detailed bedside assessment and urinalysis definitively ruled out systemic sepsis and acute internal organ involvement (renal/bowel). The findings confirmed a mild, post-infectious cutaneous vasculitis. This urgent assessment safely averted an unnecessary lights-and-sirens emergency department admission and intravenous antibiotic administration, allowing the highly anxious patient to remain safely at home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-infectious nature of the rash. Bedside urinalysis performed to rule out renal involvement.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep your legs elevated on a pillow whenever you are sitting or sleeping over the next 48 hours. Elevating your legs decreases the hydrostatic pressure in the tiny blood vessels, which will directly stop the rash from spreading and speed up the fading process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 3-5 days for formal urinalysis and blood pressure check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe, cramping abdominal pain, if you begin vomiting, or if your urine turns the color of cola or tea.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This is a temporary inflammation of the very small blood vessels in the skin, triggered by a recent cold. It looks alarming but usually resolves spontaneously with rest.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the spots spread rapidly up your body, if you develop a high fever, or if your joints become too painful to walk.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The rash is caused by tiny drops of blood leaking from irritated vessels under the skin due to gravity. It is not a dangerous bacterial infection.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely tomorrow. Avoid standing for long periods. Follow up with your GP to ensure your kidneys remain unaffected.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: IgA Vasculitis (mild blood vessel inflammation). No signs of dangerous infection. Urine test normal. Keep legs elevated. See GP in 3 days.

